---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import MarkdownToWordTool from '../../components/tools/MarkdownToWordTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { parseMarkdown, renderHtml, summarize, SAMPLE_MARKDOWN } from '../../lib/markdown';
import { markdownToDocx } from '../../lib/markdown-to-docx';

const tool = toolBySlug('markdown-to-word');

// Every fact below is produced by the real engine at build time.
const sampleBytes = markdownToDocx(SAMPLE_MARKDOWN, { title: 'Sample document' });
const sampleKb = Math.round((sampleBytes.length / 1024) * 10) / 10;
const sampleBlocks = parseMarkdown(SAMPLE_MARKDOWN);
const stats = summarize(sampleBlocks);
const sampleHtmlPreview = renderHtml(sampleBlocks);
if (sampleBytes.length < 5000) throw new Error('sample .docx looks implausibly small — engine may have produced an empty document');

const nestedList = '- Parent item\n  - Child item\n    1. Grandchild, numbered\n    2. Second grandchild';
const nestedBytes = markdownToDocx(nestedList);
if (nestedBytes.length < 3000) throw new Error('nested-list .docx looks too small');

const restartList = '1. First list, item one\n2. First list, item two\n\nSome text between them.\n\n1. Second list, item one\n2. Second list, item two';
const restartBytes = markdownToDocx(restartList);
if (restartBytes.length < 3000) throw new Error('restart-list .docx looks too small');

const emptyBytes = markdownToDocx('');
if (emptyBytes.length < 2000) throw new Error('page claims empty input still produces a valid, openable .docx');

const steps = [
  'Paste the Markdown into the left box. The preview on the right shows headings, bold, lists, tables, code and links as you type.',
  'Click <strong>Download .docx</strong> (or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> in the input box).',
  'Open the downloaded file directly in Word, LibreOffice, or drag it into Google Docs / drive.google.com to open it there: no copy-paste step, no intermediate format.',
  'Need it on the clipboard instead of as a file? The companion <a href="/tools/markdown-to-google-docs">Markdown to Google Docs</a> tool pastes formatted text directly into an already-open document.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How is this different from Markdown to Google Docs?',
    a: 'That tool puts formatted text on your clipboard for you to paste into a document you already have open. This one builds an actual .docx file and downloads it, the thing to reach for when you need a file to attach to an email, upload somewhere, or hand to someone who expects a Word document rather than a pasted block of text.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this a real Word document, or Markdown wrapped in a .docx extension?',
    a: `A real one: the file is assembled by hand as a package of WordprocessingML XML (the same format Word itself writes), not text dumped into a renamed file. Headings use Word's own Heading 1-6 styles, lists use real Word numbering (so they show up in Word's own list tools, not just as plain text with a leading dash), and tables are genuine Word tables with borders, not an image or a monospace approximation. The sample Markdown below becomes a ${sampleKb} KB .docx with ${stats.headings} heading${stats.headings === 1 ? '' : 's'}, ${stats.lists} list${stats.lists === 1 ? '' : 's'} and ${stats.tables} table${stats.tables === 1 ? '' : 's'}, all real Word elements.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this upload my document anywhere to convert it?',
    a: 'No. The entire .docx file (every XML part, the ZIP container itself) is assembled by JavaScript running in your browser. Nothing is sent to a server; this page could be disconnected from the network entirely and still produce a working file.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do nested and numbered lists come out right, not just visually indented?',
    a: 'Yes. This was worth getting right on purpose. Two separate numbered lists in the same document each restart at 1 rather than continuing each other\'s count (a common bug in hand-rolled Word generators, since Word numbering is tracked by an ID that has to be allocated fresh per list); nested lists indent to their actual depth, with bullet lists cycling through different bullet characters by level the way Word\'s own nested-list styles do.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <MarkdownToWordTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>A file, not a paste</h2>
    <p>
      Most "Markdown to Word" tools on the web actually convert to HTML and rely on your browser's or
      Word's own paste-formatting to turn that into something Word-shaped once you paste it in, which
      works, but only inside a document you already have open. This tool builds the <code>.docx</code>
      file itself: a ZIP archive of WordprocessingML XML, the exact format Word saves, assembled entirely
      in your browser with no external library and no server round trip.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>The sample Markdown below converts to a {sampleKb} KB <code>.docx</code>, {stats.words} words,
      {stats.headings} headings, {stats.lists} lists and {stats.tables} table across real Word styles and elements:</p>
    <div class="example-preview" set:html={sampleHtmlPreview} />

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Load sample</strong> fills the input with the Markdown shown above, so there's something to try immediately.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear</strong> empties the input and resets the preview.</li>
      <li><strong>Download .docx</strong> builds the file fresh from the current Markdown and saves it; there is no separate "convert" step, since the preview already reflects exactly what will download.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Images become a labelled link, not an embedded picture.</strong> <code>![alt](url)</code>
        produces a hyperlink reading "[Image: alt text]" rather than the actual picture. Embedding a
        fetched image as a real Word media part is real added scope (a network fetch, format sniffing, a
        relationship and drawing XML per image) that this pass didn't take on. If you need the image
        itself in the document, add it in Word after downloading.</li>
      <li><strong>Nested numbered lists restart correctly.</strong> See the FAQ: this is the one thing
        most hand-rolled Word generators get wrong (verified here with a dedicated test for exactly that
        case).</li>
      <li><strong>Footnotes, definition lists and Markdown extensions</strong> outside GitHub-flavored
        Markdown (the same engine <a href="/tools/markdown-to-google-docs">Markdown to Google Docs</a>
        and <a href="/tools/markdown-to-plain-text">Markdown to Plain Text</a> use) are not recognised:
        headings, paragraphs, bold/italic/strikethrough/code, links, lists (including task list
        checkboxes), block quotes, fenced code blocks, tables and horizontal rules all convert; anything
        else is treated as plain text.</li>
      <li><strong>Empty input still downloads a valid, openable file</strong> rather than erroring (a
        blank one-page document, the same as opening Word and saving immediately).</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing is kept after you close the tab.</strong> The file exists only as long as your
        browser holds it in memory before the download completes; reload the page and it's gone, the same
        as every other tool on this site.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same thing in code</h2>
    <pre><code>{`# Python (python-docx, a third-party library: pip install python-docx)
from docx import Document
doc = Document()
doc.add_heading("Title", level=1)
doc.add_paragraph("Some text.")
doc.save("out.docx")

// This tool takes a different approach on purpose: no dependency, built by hand
// as a ZIP of WordprocessingML XML (see src/lib/markdown-to-docx.ts in the repo),
// so it runs entirely client-side with nothing to install.`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      Already have the result in a document rather than Markdown? <a href="/tools/google-docs-to-markdown">Google Docs to Markdown</a>
      converts the other way. Need the formatted text on your clipboard instead of a file? <a href="/tools/markdown-to-google-docs">Markdown to Google Docs</a> is the paste-first version of this same conversion.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>

<style>
  .example-preview {
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); background: var(--surface);
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.55; margin: 0 0 1rem;
    /* The injected sample can include a table wider than a narrow viewport — same fix as the
       site's own .table-wrap utility, applied to the whole box since the content is one HTML
       blob (heading + prose + table) rather than a table we could wrap in isolation. Found in
       the 2026-09-16 mobile UX audit: a real ~80px horizontal overflow at 320px. */
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .example-preview :global(h1) { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0.6em 0 0.35em; }
  .example-preview :global(h2) { font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 0.6em 0 0.35em; }
  .example-preview :global(h1:first-child), .example-preview :global(h2:first-child) { margin-top: 0; }
  .example-preview :global(p) { margin: 0 0 0.75em; }
  .example-preview :global(ul), .example-preview :global(ol) { margin: 0 0 0.75em; padding-left: 1.5em; }
  .example-preview :global(table) { margin: 0 0 0.75em; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .example-preview :global(th), .example-preview :global(td) { padding: 0.3rem 0.55rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .example-preview :global(th) { background: var(--surface-2); text-align: left; }
  .example-preview :global(pre) { background: var(--surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem; font-size: 0.85rem; margin: 0 0 0.75em; overflow-x: auto; }
  .example-preview :global(code) { font-size: 0.9em; background: var(--surface-2); border-radius: 3px; padding: 0.05em 0.3em; }
  .example-preview :global(pre code) { background: none; padding: 0; }
</style>
